.TH SVR4 4 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
svr4 \- UNIX System V Release 4 execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B SVR4
personality
.RB ( PERS_SVR4 )
runs i386 ELF programs built for UNIX System V/386 Release 4: AT&T's
reference port and the releases made from it.  It is rooted at
.IR /perso/svr4 ,
which is where a program's absolute paths, its interpreter and its
libraries are looked for first.
.SS Recognition
A Release 4 binary carries nothing that names it: its ELF OS/ABI byte is 0
and it has no note.  The ELF loader gives a 32-bit program this personality
when
.IP \(bu 2
its interpreter is
.IR /usr/lib/libc.so.1 ,
Release 4.0's libc being its own dynamic linker; or
.IP \(bu
it is statically linked and unbranded, and either lies under
.I /perso/svr4
or is run by a process that is already SVR4.
.PP
The dynamic linker inside
.I libc.so.1
is the vendor's and runs as shipped.  It is started the System V way, from
the auxiliary vector, and gets its memory by mapping
.BR zero (4).
.SS System call interface
A system call is
.B "lcall $7,$0"
with the number in
.B EAX
and the arguments on the stack above the stub's return address.  Carry
reports failure with the errno in
.BR EAX ;
a second result is returned in
.BR EDX .
This is also the interface of the 32-bit half of
.BR xenix (4);
the entry code and the calls numbered below 64 are common to both and
belong to neither.  Xenix's own multiplexed call 40 is not provided.
There is no call gate behind selector 7, so the call faults and is
emulated from the fault.  No Release 4 program issues
.BR "int $0x80" ,
and the personality has no table for it.
.PP
Beyond the shared calls, these are implemented:
.BR sysi86 " (" SI86FPHW ),
.BR fsync ,
.BR rmdir ,
.BR mkdir ,
.BR getdents ,
.BR symlink ,
.BR readlink ,
.BR fchmod ,
.BR fchown ,
.BR sigprocmask ,
.BR sigsuspend ,
.BR sigaction ,
.BR sigpending ,
.BR waitsys ,
.BR mmap ,
.BR mprotect ,
.BR munmap ,
.BR vfork ,
.BR fchdir ,
.BR xstat ,
.BR lxstat ,
.BR fxstat ,
.BR setrlimit ,
.BR getrlimit ,
.BR lchown ,
.BR rename ,
.BR uname ,
.BR setegid ,
.BR seteuid ,
.BR sysconfig ,
.BR systeminfo ,
and
.BR pgrpsys .
A call that is not implemented fails with
.BR ENOSYS .
.SS Translation
Signal numbers, signal sets, the
.B sigaction
flags,
.B open
flags, the expanded
.B "struct stat"
of
.BR xstat ,
.BR "struct termios" ,
the
.B siginfo
of
.BR waitid ,
resource numbers and the error numbers that differ are translated.
.B ioctl
requests are never passed through as they are, because Release 4's numbers
collide with substrate's: the
.B termio
and
.B termios
requests, the window size and the terminal's process group are translated,
and anything else fails with
.BR ENOTTY .
.SS Networking
Release 4 has no socket system calls: a program opens a STREAMS transport
provider and exchanges Transport Provider Interface messages with it, and
.BR socket (),
.BR connect ()
and
.BR t_connect ()
are library functions written on that.
The personality provides the providers
.IR /dev/tcp ,
.IR /dev/udp ,
.IR /dev/ticots ,
.I /dev/ticotsord
and
.I /dev/ticlts
over substrate's sockets, with the
.BR sockmod ,
.B timod
and
.B tirdwr
modules, the
.B I_
and
.B TI_
.B ioctl
requests,
.BR getmsg ,
.BR putmsg ,
.BR poll ,
.B readv
and
.BR writev .
A program can connect, exchange data and close; it cannot listen.
The file a local socket is bound to is reported by
.B stat
as a FIFO with no permission bits, which is what Release 4's
.BR connect ()
requires of it.
.PP
The local transports of the X Window System are provided as well:
.IR /dev/X/server. N,
and the Release 3 stream pipe
.I /dev/spx
with
.IR /dev/X N R .
Each is a connection to substrate's X server at
.IR /tmp/.X11-unix/X N.
.I docs/specs/sysv_streams_transport.md
describes all of it.
.SS Terminals
.I /dev
is substrate's own for this personality, whatever the tree under
.I /perso/svr4
has there, so a program that lists it to find its terminal's name sees the
terminals there are.
.PP
A pseudo-terminal is taken the System V way: the master is
.IR /dev/ptmx ,
.BR ptsname ()
asks it
.RB ( I_STR ,
.BR ISPTM )
and names the slave
.IR /dev/pts/ N
from the minor number
.B fstat
reports,
.BR grantpt ()
runs the system's own
.IR /usr/lib/pt_chmod ,
and
.BR unlockpt ()
is
.BR I_STR ,
.BR UNLKPT .
The slave is a terminal already; pushing
.BR ptem ,
.B ldterm
or
.B ttcompat
onto it succeeds and changes nothing.
The requests of
.B ttcompat
\(em
.BR TIOCGETP ,
.BR TIOCSETP ,
.BR TIOCGETC ,
.BR TIOCGLTC ,
.B TIOCLGET
and the rest of the 4.3BSD terminal interface \(em are carried out on any
terminal.
.I docs/specs/sysv_pseudo_terminals.md
describes all of it.
.SS Signals
A handler is entered as
.IR handler ( signo ", " siginfo ", " ucontext )
with libc's trampoline as its return address;
.B sigaction
is handed the trampoline in
.BR EDX .
The trampoline returns through
.BR "lcall $0xf,$0" .
.I siginfo
and
.I ucontext
are null pointers.
.SH DEBUGGING
.B debug=perso:svr4:syscall
on the kernel command line traces every call with its arguments and
result, marking the ones not implemented.
.B debug=perso:svr4:streams
adds the transport messages, in hexadecimal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/svr4
The personality's root.
.I tools/svr4/build-image.sh
builds an image of it from a set of distribution floppies, which are not
part of substrate.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_svr4.c
The personality.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/svr4/svr4_calls.c
Its system calls.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/svr4/svr4_streams.c
The STREAMS transport providers and the X transports.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/svr4/svr4_tty.c
Pseudo-terminals and the terminal modules.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_sysv386.c
The entry convention, signal frame and calls it has in common with
Xenix/386.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR context " (" getcontext ", " setcontext ),
.BR sigaltstack ,
System V IPC,
.BR mount ,
.BR statvfs ,
.BR memcntl
and
.B priocntl
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
A handler installed with
.B SA_SIGINFO
is given null
.I siginfo
and
.I ucontext
pointers.
.IP \(bu
.B ps
reports that it cannot find a controlling terminal.
.IP \(bu
Only ELF programs are recognised.  Release 4 also runs Release 3 COFF
binaries, which the
.B SVR3
personality claims instead.
.SH VERIFIED
Against Intel's UNIX System V/386 Release 4.0 Version 2: the statically
linked
.I /sbin/sh
and the dynamically linked
.BR ksh ,
.BR ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR date ,
.BR id ,
.BR uname ,
.BR sort ,
.BR wc ,
.BR grep ,
.BR sed ,
.BR expr
and
.BR head ,
with pipes, command substitution, redirection, traps and exit statuses, on
the i386 and x86_64 kernels.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xenix (4),
.BR zero (4),
.BR execve (2)
.SH HISTORY
The personality was a table of native calls that nothing selected until
October 2026, when it was wired to the ELF loader and given its own calls.
